      "math_academy_id": "q-76067",
      "knowledge_point_id": "195aa6d7-fbb5-5b8f-8c0f-0df7a844ad59",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Intervals on Which a Radical Function Is Continuous",
      "problem": "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{6+x}+\\sqrt{5x+10}$ is continuous.",
      "worked_solution": "Our function consists of two radical functions, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{6+x}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6+x\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,x\\ge -6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{5x+10}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+10\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,5x\\ge -10\\,⟹\\,x\\ge -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe domain of $f(x)$ is given by the intersection of the domain of our radical functions:\n\n$$\n[-6,\\infty )\\cap [-2,\\infty )=[-2,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on this interval.",
      "answer_fields": [
